Below is a **drop‑in** solution that gives every form field a *type‑writer* animation that runs at **300 WPM** (≈ 25 characters / s).  
The animation starts immediately when the user types the first character – no extra delay is needed, but you can add a small `animation-delay` if you want a pause before the cursor appears.

---

## 1️⃣  CSS – Custom cursor + typing animation

```css
/* ── 1. Cursor blink ─────────────────────────────────────── */
@keyframes blink {
  0%, 50% { opacity: 1; }
  51%, 100% { opacity: 0; }
}

/* ── 2. Base field styling ─────────────────────────────────── */
.typewriter {
  font-family: monospace;
  font-size: 1rem;
  color: #333;
  border: none;
  outline: none;
  background: transparent;
  padding: 0.5rem 0.5rem;
  width: 100%;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  caret-color: transparent;          /* hide the native caret */
  position: relative;
}

/* ── 3. Custom blinking cursor ─────────────────────────────── */
.typewriter::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  right: 0.5rem;
  top: 50%;
  transform: translateY(-50%);
  width: 2px;
  height: 1.2em;
  background: #333;
  animation: blink 1s step-end infinite;
}

/* ── 4. “Typing” effect – reveal text from data‑typed ─────────── */
.typewriter::before {
  content: attr(data-typed);
  position: absolute;
  left: 0.5rem;
  top: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow: hidden;
  /* hide the cursor inside the pseudo‑element */
  border-right: 2px solid transparent;
  /* animation – duration & steps are set via CSS variables */
  animation: typing var(--typing-duration) steps(var(--typing-steps)) forwards;
  animation-delay: var(--typing-delay, 0s);   /* optional delay */
}

@keyframes typing {
  from { width: 0; }
  to   { width: 100%; }
}
```

> **Why 25 chars / s?**  
> 300 WPM × 5 chars/word ≈ 1500 chars/min ≈ 25 chars/s.  
> So each character takes `1/25 s = 0.04 s`.  
> We’ll set the animation duration to `0.04 s × steps`.

---

## 2️⃣  JavaScript – Hook the animation to user input

```js
/**
 * Attach a typewriter effect to all inputs/textarea with the class `.typewriter`.
 * The effect starts on the first keystroke and runs at 300 WPM (≈ 25 chars/s).
 */
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const fields = document.querySelectorAll('.typewriter');

  fields.forEach(field => {
    // Preserve the original placeholder (if any)
    const placeholder = field.getAttribute('placeholder') || '';
    field.setAttribute('data-typed', placeholder);

    field.addEventListener('input', () => {
      const value = field.value;
      field.setAttribute('data-typed', value);

      // Only start the animation on the first character
      if (value.length === 1) {
        const steps = value.length + 1;          // +1 for the cursor
        const duration = steps * 0.04;           // 0.04 s per char
        field.style.setProperty('--typing-steps', steps);
        field.style.setProperty('--typing-duration', `${duration}s`);
        // Optional: add a small delay before the cursor appears
        // field.style.setProperty('--typing-delay', '0.5s');
      }
    });

    // Reset when the field is cleared
    field.addEventListener('blur', () => {
      if (!field.value) {
        field.setAttribute('data-typed', placeholder);
        field.style.removeProperty('--typing-steps');
        field.style.removeProperty('--typing-duration');
        // field.style.removeProperty('--typing-delay'); // if you set it
      }
    });
  });
});
```

### What the script does

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **1** | On page load, find every `.typewriter` field. |
| **2** | Store the placeholder in `data-typed` so the cursor shows the hint. |
| **3** | On the first keystroke, calculate `steps` (characters + cursor) and set the CSS variables `--typing-steps` and `--typing-duration`. |
| **4** | The CSS animation then reveals the text at 25 chars/s. |
| **5** | When the field loses focus and is empty, the animation resets. |

